Ladybird preserves intrinsic width and height keywords on
non-replaced boxes with a preferred aspect ratio. Only replaced boxes
use the natural-size exception that treats an intrinsic keyword as
auto when the opposite natural dimension is absent. Column flex items
measure fit-content height at their own resolved inline size, using
the same helper as their min-content and max-content measurements.

Blink resolves intrinsic inline keywords in
ComputeInlineSizeForFragmentInternal before applying size bounds.
FlexLayoutAlgorithm::ConstructAndAppendFlexItems obtains the item’s
own geometry with CalculateInitialFragmentGeometry, and transfers a
resolvable size through BlockSizeFromAspectRatio when appropriate.

Gecko’s nsIFrame::ComputeISizeValueFromExtremumLength uses intrinsic
inline sizes when the opposite axis can’t supply a definite size.
nsFlexContainerFrame::MeasureFlexItemContentBSize measures the child
with shrink-wrapping, overriding its inline size for stretched items.
This likewise distinguishes the item’s width from its container’s.

WebKit’s RenderBox::computeSizingKeywordLogicalWidthUsing and
computeIntrinsicKeywordLogicalWidths preserve intrinsic widths unless
aspect-ratio transfer applies. For block content, the flex layout
uses FlexFormattingContext::computeFlexBaseSize and the integration
helper FlexIntegrationUtils::computeBlockAxisContentSizeForFlexItem.
That helper lays out the child and uses its own logical height,
excluding its border and padding from the content measurement.

The reported non-flex reductions match Chrome at 60x64. The column
flex case with fit-content in both axes matches Chrome at 60x60,
instead of taking the container’s width and adding its own padding.
Firefox reports 64x64 for that case, so the final height intentionally
follows Chrome rather than claiming identical results in all engines.

A row-flex control with fit-content in both axes stays 60x60, while
Chrome gives 60x64. Its width already matches; the separate height
mismatch is unchanged by the column-flex measurement correction.
